\details{
\strong{Area methods (\code{auc_method}).}
\itemize{
\item \code{"linear"}: linear trapezoids everywhere.
\item \code{"linear-log"}: linear trapezoids up to the maximum and logarithmic
trapezoids afterwards.
\item \code{"linear-up-log-down"}: linear trapezoids while the concentration rises
and logarithmic trapezoids while it falls (the default).
}

A logarithmic trapezoid is used only when both concentrations are positive.

\strong{BLQ rules (\code{blq_rule}).} The rules apply when \code{lloq} is given or a BLQ
flag column is used (see \code{\link[=hp_nca]{hp_nca()}}). A sample is then below the limit of
quantification (BLQ) when it is flagged, below \code{lloq} or not positive.
Without a limit and without flags no sample is treated as BLQ. The rule
decides what number a BLQ sample stands for in the analysis:
\itemize{
\item \code{"zero"}: set to 0.
\item \code{"missing"}: left out.
\item \code{"lloq"}: set to the limit of quantification.
\item \code{"half-lloq"}: set to half of it.
\item \code{"tmax-zero-missing"}: BLQ before the maximum is set to 0, after it left
out (the WinNonlin rule, the default).
\item \code{"first-zero-mid-half-last-missing"}: BLQ before the first quantifiable
sample is 0, between quantifiable samples it is half the limit, after the
last quantifiable sample it is left out (the Monolix rule).
}

Rules that need a limit (\code{"lloq"}, \code{"half-lloq"} and the Monolix rule) stop
with a message when neither \code{lloq} is given nor the BLQ sample carries its
limit as \code{DV}.

\strong{Terminal slope.} With \code{lz_method = "best-fit"} the regression of
\code{log(concentration)} on time is fitted to the last 3, 4, 5, ... points after
Tmax (from \code{lz_min_points} to \code{lz_max_points}, 0 meaning no limit). Among the
fits with a negative slope the one with the highest adjusted R-squared is
chosen; fits within \code{lz_tolerance} of the best are considered equal and the
one with more points wins. A fit with an adjusted R-squared below
\code{lz_min_adj_r2} is rejected and no lambda z is reported. \code{"last-n"} uses
exactly the last \code{lz_points} points and \code{"time-range"} the points between
\code{lz_from} and \code{lz_to}; \code{"none"} skips the terminal phase. A terminal phase
shorter than \code{lz_min_span_ratio} half-lives is flagged (or refused with
\code{lz_require_span = TRUE}). Subject-specific choices go in \code{lz_overrides}, a
list of lists with elements among \code{id}, \code{occasion}, \code{from}, \code{to}, \code{points},
\code{exclude} (times to leave out) and \code{skip} (no terminal phase).

\strong{Extrapolated area.} When the area beyond the last sample exceeds
\code{lz_max_extrap} percent of the area to infinity the profile is flagged
(\code{lz_extrap_action = "flag"}) or its infinity-based parameters are left
empty (\code{"omit"}).

\strong{Steady state (\code{steady_state}).} \code{"auto"} uses the \code{SS} flag and \code{II} of
the data, \code{"none"} treats every interval as a single dose, \code{"last"} treats
the last interval of each subject as steady state and \code{"all"} every
interval. The dosing interval \code{tau} comes from \code{II} (with the \code{SS} flag),
from the \code{tau} argument or from the gap to the next dose when the doses are
evenly spaced. A steady-state interval reports the measures over the
dosing interval (\code{AUCTAU}, \code{CAVG} and so on) and no infinity-based
parameters.

\strong{Partial areas.} \code{partial = list(c(0, 4), c(0, 12))} adds the parameters
\code{AUC_0_4} and \code{AUC_0_12}. A partial area beyond the last sample is
extrapolated with lambda z when \code{partial_extrapolate = TRUE}.

\strong{Units.} \code{dose_unit}, \code{time_unit} and \code{conc_unit} are labels used in
summary tables; nothing is converted. \code{dose_factor} multiplies the dose in
clearance and volume, for example 1000 to turn mg and ng/mL into L/h and L.
The unit labels are not changed by it, so set them to match.
